WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:01.306 --> 00:08.166 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference March 19,

00:08.746 --> 00:11.566 align:middle
2025 CHAIR POWELL.

00:11.566 --> 00:11.876 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:12.056 --> 00:14.246 align:middle
My colleagues and I
remain squarely focused

00:14.246 --> 00:17.416 align:middle
on achieving our dual-mandate
goals of maximum employment

00:17.486 --> 00:20.436 align:middle
and stable prices for the
benefit of the American people.

00:21.186 --> 00:23.256 align:middle
The economy is strong overall

00:23.376 --> 00:25.986 align:middle
and has made significant
progress toward our goals

00:26.056 --> 00:27.316 align:middle
over the past two years.

00:28.326 --> 00:29.766 align:middle
Labor market conditions
are solid,

00:30.076 --> 00:31.676 align:middle
and inflation has moved closer

00:31.676 --> 00:33.706 align:middle
to our 2 percent
longer-run goal,

00:34.256 --> 00:35.616 align:middle
though it remains
somewhat elevated.

00:36.976 --> 00:37.946 align:middle
In support of our goals,

00:37.946 --> 00:39.886 align:middle
today the Federal Open
Market Committee decided

00:39.886 --> 00:42.186 align:middle
to leave our policy
interest rate unchanged.

00:43.156 --> 00:45.636 align:middle
We also made the technical
decision to slow the pace

00:45.856 --> 00:47.806 align:middle
of decline in the size
of our balance sheet.

00:48.866 --> 00:50.586 align:middle
I'll have more to say
about these decisions

00:50.586 --> 00:52.996 align:middle
after briefly reviewing
economic developments.

00:54.696 --> 00:57.846 align:middle
Economic activity continued
to expand at a solid pace

00:57.846 --> 00:59.336 align:middle
in the fourth quarter
of last year,

00:59.736 --> 01:01.976 align:middle
with GDP rising at 2.3 percent.

01:03.346 --> 01:05.866 align:middle
Recent indications, however,
point to a moderation

01:05.866 --> 01:08.646 align:middle
in consumer spending
following the rapid growth seen

01:08.646 --> 01:10.846 align:middle
over the second half of 2024.

01:12.216 --> 01:14.156 align:middle
Surveys of households
and businesses point

01:14.156 --> 01:16.666 align:middle
to heightened uncertainty
about the economic outlook.

01:18.086 --> 01:20.886 align:middle
It remains to be seen how these
developments might affect future

01:20.886 --> 01:22.036 align:middle
spending and investment.

01:23.916 --> 01:25.716 align:middle
In our Summary of
Economic Projections,

01:25.896 --> 01:28.146 align:middle
the median participant
projects GDP

01:28.146 --> 01:32.186 align:middle
to rise 1.7 percent this year,
somewhat lower than projected

01:32.186 --> 01:35.716 align:middle
in December, and to rise
a bit below 2 percent

01:35.716 --> 01:37.026 align:middle
over the next two years.

01:38.336 --> 01:40.346 align:middle
In the labor market,
conditions remain solid.

01:41.106 --> 01:43.826 align:middle
Payroll job gains
averaged 200,000 per month

01:43.826 --> 01:44.986 align:middle
over the past three months.

01:45.706 --> 01:49.006 align:middle
The unemployment rate, at
4.1 percent, remains low

01:49.076 --> 01:51.536 align:middle
and has held in a narrow
range for the past year.

01:52.976 --> 01:56.116 align:middle
The jobs-to-workers gap has
held steady for several months.

01:57.346 --> 01:59.426 align:middle
Wages are growing
faster than inflation

01:59.506 --> 02:01.926 align:middle
and at a more sustainable
pace than earlier

02:01.926 --> 02:03.126 align:middle
in the pandemic recovery.

02:04.796 --> 02:07.406 align:middle
Overall, a wide set
of indicators suggests

02:07.406 --> 02:10.146 align:middle
that conditions in the labor
market are broadly in balance.

02:11.496 --> 02:13.166 align:middle
The labor market is not a source

02:13.166 --> 02:15.216 align:middle
of significant inflationary
pressures.

02:16.556 --> 02:18.646 align:middle
The median projection
for the unemployment rate

02:18.646 --> 02:23.246 align:middle
in the SEP is 4.4 percent at the
end of this year and 4.3 percent

02:23.246 --> 02:24.446 align:middle
over the next two years.

02:26.416 --> 02:28.086 align:middle
Inflation has eased
significantly

02:28.086 --> 02:29.116 align:middle
over the past two years

02:29.156 --> 02:31.026 align:middle
but remains somewhat
elevated relative

02:31.026 --> 02:32.976 align:middle
to our 2 percent
longer-run goal.

02:34.286 --> 02:36.386 align:middle
Estimates based on the
consumer price index

02:36.386 --> 02:37.546 align:middle
and other data indicate

02:37.546 --> 02:40.566 align:middle
that total PCE prices
rose 2.5 percent

02:41.056 --> 02:43.676 align:middle
over the 12 months ending
in February and that,

02:43.676 --> 02:46.366 align:middle
excluding the volatile
food and energy categories,

02:46.676 --> 02:49.166 align:middle
core PCE prices rose
2.8 percent.

02:50.546 --> 02:52.256 align:middle
Some near-term measures

02:52.256 --> 02:54.596 align:middle
of inflation expectations
have recently moved up.

02:55.376 --> 02:58.076 align:middle
We see this in both market-
and survey-based measures,

02:58.616 --> 03:01.456 align:middle
and survey respondents, both
consumers and businesses,

03:01.946 --> 03:04.296 align:middle
are mentioning tariffs
as a driving factor.

03:05.156 --> 03:08.416 align:middle
Beyond the next year or
so, however, most measures

03:08.416 --> 03:10.716 align:middle
of longer-term expectations
remain consistent

03:10.716 --> 03:12.446 align:middle
with our 2 percent
inflation goal.

03:13.766 --> 03:15.216 align:middle
The median projection in the SEP

03:15.336 --> 03:18.636 align:middle
for total PCE inflation
is 2.7 percent this year

03:19.066 --> 03:21.606 align:middle
and 2.2 percent next
year, a little higher

03:21.606 --> 03:22.756 align:middle
than projected in December.

03:24.406 --> 03:28.396 align:middle
In 2027, the median projection
is at our 2 percent objective.

03:30.926 --> 03:33.996 align:middle
Our monetary policy actions
are guided by our dual mandate

03:33.996 --> 03:36.596 align:middle
to promote maximum
employment and stable prices

03:36.596 --> 03:37.616 align:middle
for the American people.

03:38.796 --> 03:40.526 align:middle
At today's meeting,
the Committee decided

03:40.526 --> 03:43.406 align:middle
to maintain the target range
for the federal funds rate

03:43.406 --> 03:45.346 align:middle
at 4¼ to 4½ percent.

03:46.346 --> 03:49.056 align:middle
Looking ahead, the new
Administration is in the process

03:49.056 --> 03:51.046 align:middle
of implementing significant
policy changes

03:51.046 --> 03:55.326 align:middle
in four distinct areas:
trade, immigration,

03:55.366 --> 03:57.176 align:middle
fiscal policy, and regulation.

03:58.116 --> 04:00.916 align:middle
It is the net effect of these
policy changes that will matter

04:00.916 --> 04:03.616 align:middle
for the economy and for the
path of monetary policy.

04:04.956 --> 04:06.536 align:middle
While there have been
recent developments in some

04:06.536 --> 04:08.516 align:middle
of these areas, especially
trade policy,

04:08.606 --> 04:10.726 align:middle
uncertainty around the
changes and their effects

04:10.726 --> 04:12.626 align:middle
on the economic outlook is high.

04:13.476 --> 04:16.376 align:middle
As we parse the incoming
information, we're focused

04:16.376 --> 04:18.326 align:middle
on separating the
signal from the noise

04:18.326 --> 04:19.796 align:middle
as the outlook evolves.

04:20.756 --> 04:23.456 align:middle
As we say in our statement,
in considering the extent

04:23.456 --> 04:25.886 align:middle
and timing of additional
adjustments to the target range

04:25.886 --> 04:26.836 align:middle
for the federal funds rate,

04:27.576 --> 04:29.426 align:middle
the Committee will
assess incoming data,

04:29.586 --> 04:31.906 align:middle
the evolving outlook,
and the balance of risks.

04:33.396 --> 04:36.146 align:middle
We do not need to be in a hurry
to adjust our policy stance,

04:36.636 --> 04:39.496 align:middle
and we are well positioned
to wait for greater clarity.

04:41.706 --> 04:44.476 align:middle
In our SEP, FOMC
participants wrote

04:44.476 --> 04:45.896 align:middle
down their individual
assessments

04:45.896 --> 04:48.006 align:middle
of an appropriate path for
the federal funds rate,

04:48.406 --> 04:50.206 align:middle
based on what each
participant judges

04:50.206 --> 04:54.216 align:middle
to be the most likely scenario
going forward-an admittedly

04:54.216 --> 04:57.306 align:middle
challenging exercise
at this time in light

04:57.306 --> 04:58.576 align:middle
of considerable uncertainty.

04:59.666 --> 05:02.306 align:middle
The median participant projects
that the appropriate level

05:02.306 --> 05:04.806 align:middle
of the federal funds rate
will be 3.9 percent at the end

05:04.806 --> 05:07.776 align:middle
of this year and 3.4 percent
at the end of next year,

05:08.736 --> 05:09.856 align:middle
unchanged from December.

05:10.986 --> 05:13.226 align:middle
While these individual
forecasts are always subject

05:13.226 --> 05:14.686 align:middle
to uncertainty, as I noted,

05:14.686 --> 05:17.046 align:middle
uncertainty today is
unusually elevated.

05:17.456 --> 05:17.936 align:middle
And, of course,

05:17.936 --> 05:20.836 align:middle
these projections are not a
Committee plan or a decision.

05:21.796 --> 05:23.616 align:middle
Policy is not on
a preset course.

05:24.346 --> 05:27.146 align:middle
As the economy evolves, we
will adjust our policy stance

05:27.266 --> 05:29.846 align:middle
in a manner that best
promotes our maximum-employment

05:30.146 --> 05:31.396 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

05:32.066 --> 05:35.536 align:middle
If the economy remains strong
and inflation does not continue

05:35.536 --> 05:37.346 align:middle
to move sustainably
toward 2 percent,

05:37.896 --> 05:40.096 align:middle
we can maintain policy
restraint for longer.

05:40.636 --> 05:42.906 align:middle
If the labor market were
to weaken unexpectedly

05:42.906 --> 05:45.196 align:middle
or inflation were to fall
more quickly than anticipated,

05:45.566 --> 05:47.166 align:middle
we can ease policy accordingly.

05:48.266 --> 05:50.906 align:middle
Our current policy stance
is well positioned to deal

05:50.906 --> 05:53.016 align:middle
with the risks and
uncertainties that we face

05:53.366 --> 05:55.796 align:middle
in pursuing both sides
of our dual mandate.

05:57.566 --> 06:00.006 align:middle
At today's meeting, we also
decided to slow the pace

06:00.006 --> 06:01.426 align:middle
of decline in our balance sheet.

06:02.066 --> 06:03.736 align:middle
Since we began balance
sheet runoff,

06:03.736 --> 06:05.376 align:middle
our securities holdings
have declined

06:05.376 --> 06:07.436 align:middle
by more than $2 trillion.

06:08.176 --> 06:11.096 align:middle
While market indicators continue
to suggest that the quantity

06:11.096 --> 06:14.166 align:middle
of reserves remains abundant,
we have seen some signs

06:14.166 --> 06:15.906 align:middle
of increased tightness
in money markets.

06:17.006 --> 06:18.756 align:middle
Beginning in April,
the monthly cap

06:18.756 --> 06:20.536 align:middle
on Treasury redemptions
will be lowered

06:20.876 --> 06:23.196 align:middle
from $25 billion to $5 billion.

06:23.816 --> 06:25.616 align:middle
Consistent with the
Committee's intention

06:25.616 --> 06:28.116 align:middle
to hold primarily Treasury
securities in the longer run,

06:28.576 --> 06:31.466 align:middle
we are leaving the cap on
agency securities unchanged.

06:32.616 --> 06:35.626 align:middle
This action has no implications
for our intended stance

06:35.626 --> 06:38.376 align:middle
of monetary policy and
should not affect the size

06:38.376 --> 06:40.296 align:middle
of our balance sheet
over the medium term.

06:40.536 --> 06:43.856 align:middle
The Committee also continued
its discussions as part

06:43.856 --> 06:46.766 align:middle
of our five-year review of
our monetary policy framework.

06:47.526 --> 06:49.876 align:middle
At this meeting, we focused
on labor market dynamics

06:49.876 --> 06:51.346 align:middle
and our maximum-employment goal.

06:52.186 --> 06:54.656 align:middle
As we have indicated, our
review will include outreach

06:54.786 --> 06:57.386 align:middle
and public events involving
a wide range of parties,

06:58.056 --> 07:00.296 align:middle
including Fed Listens
events around the country

07:00.296 --> 07:02.026 align:middle
and a research conference
in May.

07:02.926 --> 07:05.456 align:middle
Throughout this process, we
will be open to new ideas

07:05.456 --> 07:08.276 align:middle
and critical feedback, and
we will take on board lessons

07:08.276 --> 07:11.016 align:middle
of the last five years in
determining our findings.

07:11.726 --> 07:14.556 align:middle
We intend to wrap up the
review by late summer.

07:15.246 --> 07:18.556 align:middle
The Fed has been assigned two
goals for monetary policy:

07:18.756 --> 07:20.796 align:middle
maximum employment
and stable prices.

07:21.556 --> 07:23.936 align:middle
We remain committed to
supporting maximum employment,

07:24.396 --> 07:26.756 align:middle
bringing inflation sustainably
to our 2 percent goal,

07:27.166 --> 07:29.656 align:middle
and keeping longer-term
inflation expectations

07:29.706 --> 07:30.356 align:middle
well anchored.

07:31.346 --> 07:32.506 align:middle
Our success in delivering

07:32.506 --> 07:34.556 align:middle
on these goals matters
to all Americans.

07:35.276 --> 07:37.806 align:middle
We understand that our actions
affect communities, families,

07:37.806 --> 07:39.186 align:middle
and businesses across
the country.

07:39.786 --> 07:42.236 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

07:43.276 --> 07:44.906 align:middle
We at the Fed will
do everything we can

07:44.976 --> 07:46.746 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

07:46.746 --> 07:47.926 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

07:48.556 --> 07:48.916 align:middle
Thank you.

07:49.026 --> 07:50.366 align:middle
I will look forward
to your questions.

07:51.296 --> 07:53.506 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

07:53.866 --> 07:54.766 align:middle
Howard. HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

07:54.766 --> 07:56.236 align:middle
Howard Schneider with Reuters.

07:56.236 --> 07:57.846 align:middle
Thanks for your time.

07:57.846 --> 08:00.936 align:middle
So two things on sort of the
real side here, one inflation

08:00.936 --> 08:01.896 align:middle
and then-and then GDP.

08:02.276 --> 08:05.566 align:middle
How much of the higher inflation
forecast for this year is due

08:05.566 --> 08:09.386 align:middle
to tariffs, and since the
policy path remains the same,

08:09.966 --> 08:13.606 align:middle
are you effectively reading this
as a one-time price-level shock?

08:14.326 --> 08:15.096 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:16.106 --> 08:19.066 align:middle
Okay, so how much of
it is, is tariffs?

08:19.066 --> 08:23.026 align:middle
So let me say that it is
going to be very difficult

08:23.026 --> 08:26.696 align:middle
to have a precise assessment of
how much of inflation is coming

08:26.696 --> 08:27.686 align:middle
from tariffs and from,

08:27.786 --> 08:30.086 align:middle
from other-and that's
already the case.

08:30.156 --> 08:33.216 align:middle
You may have seen that
goods inflation moved

08:33.216 --> 08:35.596 align:middle
up pretty significantly in the
first two months of the year.

08:36.066 --> 08:39.986 align:middle
Trying to track that back
to actual tariff increases,

08:39.986 --> 08:42.806 align:middle
given what was tariffed and what
was not-very, very challenging.

08:42.806 --> 08:46.426 align:middle
So some of it-the, the answer is
clearly some of it-a good part

08:46.426 --> 08:49.116 align:middle
of it is, is coming
from tariffs.

08:49.156 --> 08:52.376 align:middle
But we'll be-we'll be working,
and so will other forecasters,

08:52.376 --> 08:54.166 align:middle
to try to find the
best possible way

08:54.166 --> 08:58.116 align:middle
to separate nontariff
inflation from tariff inflation.

08:59.026 --> 09:00.006 align:middle
In terms of the, the-your,

09:00.606 --> 09:02.936 align:middle
your sort of looking-through
question,

09:03.536 --> 09:05.476 align:middle
too soon to say about that.

09:05.616 --> 09:09.906 align:middle
As, as I've mentioned,
it, it can be the case

09:09.906 --> 09:12.486 align:middle
that it's appropriate sometimes
to look through inflation

09:12.486 --> 09:15.506 align:middle
if it's going to go away
quickly without action by,

09:15.806 --> 09:17.146 align:middle
by us, if it's transitory.

09:17.646 --> 09:21.636 align:middle
And that can be the case in the
case of, of tariff inflation.

09:22.106 --> 09:27.346 align:middle
I think that would depend on
the, the tariff inflation moving

09:27.346 --> 09:30.006 align:middle
through fairly quickly and would
depend critically, as well,

09:30.006 --> 09:33.166 align:middle
on inflation expectations
being well anchored-longer-term

09:33.166 --> 09:34.956 align:middle
inflation expectations
being well anchored.

09:35.416 --> 09:36.146 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

09:36.366 --> 09:38.786 align:middle
Well, I, I guess I'm looking
at the obvious here-the fact

09:38.786 --> 09:41.186 align:middle
that the policy path
doesn't change at all

09:41.186 --> 09:45.476 align:middle
and inflation is unchanged
in the outyears also.

09:46.006 --> 09:48.376 align:middle
Doesn't that imply you
all have basically decided

09:48.756 --> 09:51.276 align:middle
that there is no signal here

09:51.276 --> 09:53.016 align:middle
and that it's just
going to-we're back

09:53.016 --> 09:54.126 align:middle
to "transitory" again?

09:54.336 --> 09:54.556 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:54.556 --> 09:57.226 align:middle
So I think-I think that's
kind of the base case, but,

09:57.226 --> 09:59.196 align:middle
as I said, it's-we
really can't know that.

09:59.476 --> 10:01.776 align:middle
We're going to have to
see how things actually,

10:01.776 --> 10:02.646 align:middle
actually work out.

10:02.776 --> 10:05.366 align:middle
And the fact that there wasn't
much change-I think that's,

10:05.446 --> 10:07.256 align:middle
that's partly because, you know,

10:07.256 --> 10:10.346 align:middle
you see-you see weaker
growth but higher inflation.

10:10.346 --> 10:11.226 align:middle
They kind of offset.

10:11.436 --> 10:14.416 align:middle
And also, frankly, a little bit
of inertia when it-when it comes

10:14.416 --> 10:16.886 align:middle
to changing something in this
highly uncertain environment.

10:17.256 --> 10:19.326 align:middle
You know, you're-you, you
know, I think there is a level

10:19.326 --> 10:21.256 align:middle
of inertia where you just say,
"Maybe I'll stay where I am."

10:21.716 --> 10:23.216 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

10:23.296 --> 10:24.766 align:middle
Colby. COLBY SMITH.

10:26.106 --> 10:26.446 align:middle
Thank you.

10:26.446 --> 10:28.236 align:middle
Colby Smith with
the New York Times.

10:28.236 --> 10:30.906 align:middle
You just described inflation
expectations as well anchored,

10:30.976 --> 10:33.516 align:middle
but has your confidence in
that assessment changed at all,

10:33.836 --> 10:36.346 align:middle
given the increase in certain
measures and the high degree

10:36.346 --> 10:38.566 align:middle
of uncertainty expressed
by businesses,

10:38.566 --> 10:39.866 align:middle
households, and forecasters?

10:39.866 --> 10:40.546 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:40.806 --> 10:42.356 align:middle
So our inflation
expectations-of course,

10:42.356 --> 10:44.586 align:middle
we do monitor inflation
expectations very,

10:44.586 --> 10:48.116 align:middle
very carefully-basically,
every source we can find, and,

10:48.236 --> 10:51.416 align:middle
you know-short-term, long-term,
households, businesses,

10:51.416 --> 10:52.996 align:middle
forecasters, market-based.

10:53.516 --> 10:55.426 align:middle
And I think the picture,
broadly, is this:

10:55.426 --> 10:58.686 align:middle
You do see increases widely

10:58.766 --> 11:00.886 align:middle
in short-term inflation
expectations.

11:00.886 --> 11:04.496 align:middle
And people who fill out
surveys and answer, you know,

11:05.046 --> 11:07.676 align:middle
questionnaires are pointing
to tariffs about that.

11:08.106 --> 11:09.976 align:middle
If you look-in the survey world,

11:09.976 --> 11:13.306 align:middle
if you look a little
further out, you,

11:13.356 --> 11:15.876 align:middle
you really-you really
don't see much in the way

11:15.876 --> 11:19.386 align:middle
of an increased longer-term
inflation-inflation expectations

11:19.386 --> 11:21.016 align:middle
are mostly well anchored, if you
look at the [series maintained

11:21.016 --> 11:22.276 align:middle
by the Federal Reserve Bank
of] New York, for example.

11:23.046 --> 11:25.516 align:middle
Then you have market based,
and it's the same pattern.

11:25.516 --> 11:29.386 align:middle
You know, people and markets are
pricing in and, and breakevens,

11:29.986 --> 11:33.136 align:middle
some higher inflation over
the next year must be related

11:33.136 --> 11:33.746 align:middle
to tariffs.

11:34.326 --> 11:35.816 align:middle
We know from the surveys.

11:36.096 --> 11:39.506 align:middle
But if you look out five years
or five-year, five-year forward,

11:40.006 --> 11:43.756 align:middle
you'll see that breakevens
have-are either flat or,

11:43.756 --> 11:45.896 align:middle
actually, slightly down in
the case of a longer-term one.

11:45.896 --> 11:49.186 align:middle
So we look at that, and
we will be watching all

11:49.186 --> 11:50.366 align:middle
of it very, very carefully.

11:50.676 --> 11:52.226 align:middle
We do not take anything
for granted.

11:52.656 --> 11:54.206 align:middle
That's at the very
heart of our framework:

11:54.586 --> 11:56.046 align:middle
anchored inflation expectations.

11:56.116 --> 11:57.506 align:middle
But what-that's what
you see right now.

11:57.506 --> 11:58.226 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

11:58.226 --> 12:00.836 align:middle
And, and how much weight do
you put on the deterioration

12:00.836 --> 12:02.666 align:middle
in consumer confidence surveys?

12:03.266 --> 12:08.016 align:middle
You said recently that this is
perhaps not the best indication

12:08.016 --> 12:09.146 align:middle
of future spending.

12:09.396 --> 12:11.126 align:middle
But I'm curious, you know,

12:11.156 --> 12:13.936 align:middle
what you think is behind
this deterioration and,

12:13.936 --> 12:18.246 align:middle
and to what extent it could be a
leading indicator for hard data.

12:18.406 --> 12:18.706 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:18.706 --> 12:19.896 align:middle
So let's start with
the hard data.

12:20.096 --> 12:23.246 align:middle
You know, we, we do see
pretty solid hard data still.

12:23.466 --> 12:26.646 align:middle
So growth looks like it's
maybe moderating a bit,

12:26.676 --> 12:29.486 align:middle
consumer spending moderating a
bit, but still at a solid pace.

12:30.106 --> 12:31.836 align:middle
Unemployment's 4.1 percent.

12:32.246 --> 12:35.066 align:middle
Job creation, most recently,
has been at a healthy level.

12:35.606 --> 12:39.726 align:middle
Inflation has started to move up
now, we think partly in response

12:39.726 --> 12:44.286 align:middle
to tariffs, and there may be
a delay in further progress

12:44.336 --> 12:45.326 align:middle
over the course of this year.

12:45.766 --> 12:47.086 align:middle
So that's the hard data.

12:47.206 --> 12:48.616 align:middle
Overall, it's a solid picture.

12:49.196 --> 12:51.986 align:middle
The, the survey data, [in
the case of] both household

12:51.986 --> 12:55.656 align:middle
and businesses, show
significant rise in uncertainty

12:55.656 --> 12:58.026 align:middle
and significant concerns
about downside risks.

12:58.026 --> 12:59.106 align:middle
So how do we think about that?

12:59.106 --> 13:00.536 align:middle
And that's-that is the question.

13:01.046 --> 13:05.126 align:middle
As I mentioned the other
day, as you pointed out,

13:05.126 --> 13:07.266 align:middle
the relationship
between survey data

13:07.266 --> 13:10.006 align:middle
and actual economic activity
hasn't been very tight.

13:10.006 --> 13:11.846 align:middle
There have been plenty of times

13:11.846 --> 13:14.786 align:middle
where people are saying
very down-downbeat things

13:14.786 --> 13:16.926 align:middle
about the economy and then
going out and buying a new car.

13:17.546 --> 13:20.446 align:middle
But we don't know that
that will be the case here.

13:20.446 --> 13:22.986 align:middle
We will be watching
very carefully for signs

13:22.986 --> 13:24.286 align:middle
of weakness in the real data.

13:24.286 --> 13:25.216 align:middle
Of course we will.

13:25.696 --> 13:28.766 align:middle
But I, I-you know, given where
we are, we think our policy's

13:28.766 --> 13:32.886 align:middle
in a good place to
react to, to what comes.

13:32.956 --> 13:34.806 align:middle
And we think that the
right thing to do is,

13:34.806 --> 13:37.636 align:middle
is to wait here for, you know,
for greater clarity about,

13:38.106 --> 13:39.586 align:middle
about what, what
the economy's doing.

13:41.516 --> 13:42.696 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

13:43.036 --> 13:44.106 align:middle
Nick. NICK TIMIRAOS.

13:44.656 --> 13:45.936 align:middle
Nick Timiraos, the
Wall Street Journal.

13:46.826 --> 13:49.876 align:middle
Chair Powell, Chair Greenspan
once defined price stability as,

13:50.016 --> 13:52.746 align:middle
as an environment in which
inflation is so low and stable

13:52.746 --> 13:55.296 align:middle
over time that it
doesn't materially enter

13:55.716 --> 13:58.566 align:middle
into the decisions of
households and firms.

13:59.336 --> 14:01.166 align:middle
Can you say that today we have

14:01.166 --> 14:02.866 align:middle
that price stability-that
households

14:03.046 --> 14:05.526 align:middle
and businesses are ignoring
price growth, or do you see,

14:06.136 --> 14:09.416 align:middle
by an advanced psychology,
big changes in inventories

14:09.586 --> 14:13.046 align:middle
and surveys that show consumers,
at least in the short run,

14:13.046 --> 14:14.396 align:middle
expect higher inflation?

14:15.206 --> 14:15.526 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:15.556 --> 14:18.306 align:middle
So I, I do like that
definition a lot.

14:18.306 --> 14:21.616 align:middle
In fact, I used it at the
recent conference where I spoke.

14:21.616 --> 14:23.486 align:middle
So I think a world where,

14:23.486 --> 14:25.776 align:middle
where people can make their
daily economic decisions,

14:25.776 --> 14:28.016 align:middle
and businesses, and
they're not having to think

14:28.016 --> 14:29.186 align:middle
about the possibility

14:29.186 --> 14:31.406 align:middle
of significantly high
inflation-we know inflation will

14:31.406 --> 14:31.856 align:middle
bounce around.

14:31.856 --> 14:33.326 align:middle
That's, that is price stability.

14:33.326 --> 14:35.876 align:middle
You know, I think we
were getting closer

14:35.876 --> 14:37.066 align:middle
and closer to that.

14:37.276 --> 14:38.686 align:middle
I wouldn't say we were at that.

14:39.186 --> 14:42.576 align:middle
Inflation was running around
2½ percent for some time.

14:43.126 --> 14:46.706 align:middle
I do think with the arrival
of, of the tariff inflation,

14:47.926 --> 14:49.646 align:middle
further progress may be delayed,

14:49.646 --> 14:52.626 align:middle
if the SEP doesn't really
show further downward progress

14:52.626 --> 14:53.526 align:middle
on inflation this year.

14:53.526 --> 14:56.556 align:middle
And that, that's really due
to the tariffs coming in.

14:56.646 --> 14:59.496 align:middle
So, delayed, but if you
look at our forecast,

14:59.556 --> 15:04.186 align:middle
we do see ourselves getting back
into the low 2s in '26 and then

15:04.186 --> 15:06.436 align:middle
down to 2 [percent] by '27-of
course, highly uncertain.

15:06.436 --> 15:10.326 align:middle
So I see progress having,
having been made toward that,

15:10.496 --> 15:11.716 align:middle
and then progress in the future.

15:11.716 --> 15:13.336 align:middle
I think that progress
is probably delayed

15:13.336 --> 15:13.956 align:middle
for the time being.

15:14.396 --> 15:15.136 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

15:15.136 --> 15:18.296 align:middle
If that's the case, why are
there cuts in the SEP for 2025?

15:18.386 --> 15:18.916 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:19.526 --> 15:22.736 align:middle
So, again, people wrote
down two cuts the last time,

15:22.906 --> 15:24.556 align:middle
and they look at-they
look at the-you know,

15:24.556 --> 15:29.136 align:middle
the-and they wrote down,
you know, meaningful decline

15:29.136 --> 15:34.026 align:middle
in growth out from
2.1 to 1.7 in 2025,

15:34.416 --> 15:36.906 align:middle
a tick up in the unemployment
rate, so not much there,

15:37.326 --> 15:39.206 align:middle
but core inflation
up by three-tenths.

15:39.206 --> 15:42.176 align:middle
And so those two kind of-those
kind of balance each other out.

15:42.176 --> 15:44.686 align:middle
So people-not everybody,
but, on balance,

15:45.206 --> 15:46.936 align:middle
people wrote down
similar numbers.

15:46.936 --> 15:48.026 align:middle
The changes aren't that big.

15:48.426 --> 15:50.216 align:middle
The other-the other factor,
though, as I mentioned,

15:50.216 --> 15:51.946 align:middle
is just really high uncertainty.

15:52.386 --> 15:53.926 align:middle
What would you write down?

15:54.046 --> 15:55.726 align:middle
I mean, it's just-it's
really hard

15:55.726 --> 15:58.056 align:middle
to know how this is
going to work out.

15:58.606 --> 16:01.056 align:middle
And, again, we think our
policy's in a good place.

16:01.056 --> 16:02.996 align:middle
We think it's a good
place where we can move

16:02.996 --> 16:04.876 align:middle
in the direction
that-where we need to.

16:04.876 --> 16:07.816 align:middle
But in the meantime, we,
we-it's, it's really appropriate

16:07.816 --> 16:10.956 align:middle
to wait for further clarity,
and, of course, you know,

16:10.956 --> 16:12.066 align:middle
the costs of doing that,

16:12.066 --> 16:15.196 align:middle
given that the economy is
still solid, are very low.

16:17.176 --> 16:18.376 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

16:19.716 --> 16:20.366 align:middle
Edward. EDWARD LAWRENCE.

16:20.366 --> 16:21.126 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

16:21.306 --> 16:22.416 align:middle
Edward Lawrence with
Fox Business.

16:22.416 --> 16:24.996 align:middle
So with near 4 percent
unemployment rate,

16:25.166 --> 16:26.536 align:middle
that should be low
enough to bring people

16:26.536 --> 16:28.726 align:middle
in from the sidelines
in terms of hiring.

16:29.116 --> 16:31.486 align:middle
But we're seeing the
hiring rates have been stuck

16:31.486 --> 16:33.326 align:middle
at 2023, 2024 levels.

16:33.326 --> 16:35.026 align:middle
So, what's going on there?

16:35.026 --> 16:36.246 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:36.246 --> 16:38.706 align:middle
Yeah, so that's a feature of
the-has been for some time,

16:38.706 --> 16:39.946 align:middle
a feature of this labor market.

16:39.986 --> 16:42.106 align:middle
You have pretty high
participation,

16:42.106 --> 16:43.426 align:middle
accounting for aging.

16:43.856 --> 16:45.816 align:middle
You've got wages
that are consistent

16:45.816 --> 16:48.096 align:middle
with 2 percent inflation,
assuming that we're going

16:48.096 --> 16:51.066 align:middle
to keep getting, you know,
relatively high productivity.

16:51.096 --> 16:52.676 align:middle
We've got unemployment,
you know,

16:52.676 --> 16:55.036 align:middle
pretty close to its
natural level.

16:55.766 --> 17:00.466 align:middle
But job-the hiring rate is quite
low, but so is the layoff rate.

17:00.656 --> 17:03.216 align:middle
So the, the-you look at
initial claims or layoffs.

17:03.546 --> 17:05.626 align:middle
So you're not seeing
people losing their jobs,

17:05.956 --> 17:07.896 align:middle
but you're seeing that people
who don't have a job having

17:07.896 --> 17:09.086 align:middle
to wait longer and longer.

17:09.596 --> 17:11.796 align:middle
And, you know, the question
is, which way does that break?

17:11.796 --> 17:15.296 align:middle
If we were to see a
meaningful increase in layoffs,

17:15.296 --> 17:17.776 align:middle
then that would probably
translate fairly quickly

17:18.106 --> 17:21.136 align:middle
into unemployment, because
people are-you know, it's,

17:21.136 --> 17:23.326 align:middle
it's not a-it's not
a big hiring market.

17:24.006 --> 17:26.196 align:middle
We've been watching that, and
it's just not in the data.

17:26.196 --> 17:26.926 align:middle
It hasn't happened.

17:26.926 --> 17:28.926 align:middle
What we've had is,
is a low-firing,

17:28.926 --> 17:32.806 align:middle
low-hiring situation, and it
seems to be in balance now for,

17:33.396 --> 17:35.326 align:middle
you know, for the last
six, seven, eight months.

17:35.796 --> 17:37.186 align:middle
That's where we are.

17:37.426 --> 17:39.606 align:middle
There's healthy levels
of job creation, too.

17:40.116 --> 17:42.626 align:middle
So, overall, it's a labor
market that's in balance, and,

17:42.626 --> 17:44.176 align:middle
you know, we, we watch
it very carefully.

17:44.176 --> 17:44.726 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

17:44.876 --> 17:46.906 align:middle
So then, have we seen the
Administration-the new

17:46.906 --> 17:49.566 align:middle
Administration's policies
in the economic numbers yet?

17:49.876 --> 17:51.046 align:middle
And when do you anticipate
that happening?

17:51.286 --> 17:51.536 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:51.536 --> 17:52.486 align:middle
In labor? Or in, in other-?

17:52.486 --> 17:52.886 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

17:52.886 --> 17:55.156 align:middle
In labor and inflation,
just across the economy.

17:55.156 --> 17:57.446 align:middle
Have we started to see the
new policies take effect

17:57.506 --> 17:58.266 align:middle
in the numbers?

17:59.136 --> 17:59.436 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:59.846 --> 18:02.986 align:middle
I, you know, only in
a-kind of an early way.

18:03.066 --> 18:04.566 align:middle
I mean, it's only been
a few months, right?

18:04.716 --> 18:06.926 align:middle
You know, for example, the,

18:06.996 --> 18:10.986 align:middle
the layoffs that are
happening here are, you know,

18:10.986 --> 18:12.936 align:middle
they're certainly meaningful
to the people involved,

18:12.936 --> 18:15.366 align:middle
and they may be meaningful
to a particular neighborhood

18:15.366 --> 18:18.296 align:middle
or region or area, but
at the national level,

18:18.296 --> 18:19.756 align:middle
they're not-they're
not significant yet.

18:19.796 --> 18:20.336 align:middle
But we don't know.

18:20.586 --> 18:22.716 align:middle
We don't know what-where
that-how far that will go.

18:22.716 --> 18:23.976 align:middle
We'll, we'll find out much more.

18:24.636 --> 18:27.306 align:middle
I mentioned that-you,
you saw it-we've had two,

18:28.166 --> 18:31.916 align:middle
two very strong goods inflation
readings in the last two months,

18:31.916 --> 18:34.476 align:middle
which is very unexpected,
and, I think,

18:34.956 --> 18:36.936 align:middle
hard to trace it to
specific tariffs.

18:36.936 --> 18:38.736 align:middle
But it must have-it
must have something

18:38.736 --> 18:42.276 align:middle
to do with-it's either
noise and it'll come back,

18:42.276 --> 18:43.726 align:middle
and that-that's very
possible, too.

18:43.726 --> 18:46.506 align:middle
But if it is persistent, then
it must be to do with, you know,

18:46.896 --> 18:49.546 align:middle
people buying ahead of
tariffs, or raising prices ahead

18:49.546 --> 18:50.746 align:middle
of tariffs, and things
like that.

18:50.746 --> 18:52.896 align:middle
That-those kinds of things
happen, and they're very,

18:52.896 --> 18:56.066 align:middle
very hard to capture because
so much of it is indirect.

18:56.436 --> 19:00.226 align:middle
A great example is there-washing
machines were tariffed

19:00.226 --> 19:03.416 align:middle
in the last, last round of
tariffs, and prices went up,

19:03.416 --> 19:07.966 align:middle
but prices also went up on, on
dryers, which were not tariffed.

19:08.436 --> 19:10.446 align:middle
So the manufacturers just,
you know, they just kind

19:10.446 --> 19:12.256 align:middle
of followed the crowd
and, and raised it.

19:12.256 --> 19:14.976 align:middle
So things happen
very indirectly,

19:15.296 --> 19:17.746 align:middle
and so there'll be a lot of work
done in the coming months to,

19:17.936 --> 19:19.316 align:middle
to try to trace all
that through.

19:19.316 --> 19:20.926 align:middle
But, ultimately,
though, it's too soon

19:20.926 --> 19:22.966 align:middle
to be seeing significant
effects in economic data.

19:22.966 --> 19:23.426 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

19:24.856 --> 19:27.306 align:middle
Craig. CRAIG TORRES.

19:27.456 --> 19:29.126 align:middle
Craig Torres from
Bloomberg News.

19:30.126 --> 19:30.956 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

19:30.956 --> 19:33.136 align:middle
You said transitory
price increases

19:33.136 --> 19:36.296 align:middle
from tariffs are the base
case-transitory is the

19:36.326 --> 19:36.896 align:middle
base case.

19:37.556 --> 19:40.676 align:middle
Wasn't it the base
case last time?

19:40.676 --> 19:44.946 align:middle
And didn't the FOMC forecast
lower inflation ahead last time?

19:45.546 --> 19:48.506 align:middle
And wasn't the lesson that
it quickly got into services,

19:48.506 --> 19:50.306 align:middle
haircuts, daycare,
everything else?

19:51.146 --> 19:54.496 align:middle
And so I'm just wondering
why the nine aren't taking

19:54.496 --> 19:57.896 align:middle
that on board and are
cutting twice this year.

19:57.896 --> 19:58.626 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:58.626 --> 19:59.996 align:middle
When you say "last
time," are you talking

19:59.996 --> 20:00.786 align:middle
about the last- CRAIG TORRES.

20:00.786 --> 20:01.826 align:middle
Pandemic, yes-during
the pandemic.

20:01.826 --> 20:02.046 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:02.046 --> 20:02.656 align:middle
You could have been talking

20:02.656 --> 20:04.056 align:middle
about the last time
there were tariffs.

20:04.056 --> 20:04.356 align:middle
CRAIG TORRES.

20:04.356 --> 20:04.656 align:middle
Okay. CHAIR POWELL.

20:04.656 --> 20:06.906 align:middle
In which case, the
inflation was, was transitory.

20:06.906 --> 20:07.356 align:middle
CRAIG TORRES.

20:07.756 --> 20:08.246 align:middle
Yeah. CHAIR POWELL.

20:08.246 --> 20:10.016 align:middle
Yeah. No-of course,
we're well aware of that.

20:10.016 --> 20:13.796 align:middle
And, you know, it's,
it's still the truth, if,

20:14.376 --> 20:18.056 align:middle
if there's an inflationary
impulse that's going to go away

20:18.056 --> 20:22.896 align:middle
on its own, it's not the right
policy to, to tighten policy,

20:23.146 --> 20:25.366 align:middle
because by the time you have
your effect, you're-you know,

20:25.366 --> 20:27.476 align:middle
you're-an effect
that by-by design,

20:27.916 --> 20:30.916 align:middle
you are lowering economic
activity and employment.

20:31.246 --> 20:33.296 align:middle
And if that's not necessary,
you don't want to do it.

20:33.496 --> 20:37.416 align:middle
In real time, as we know, it's
hard to make that judgment.

20:37.416 --> 20:41.486 align:middle
So-and, and we're well aware,
you know, of what happened,

20:41.486 --> 20:45.096 align:middle
obviously, with, with
the pandemic inflation.

20:45.526 --> 20:47.746 align:middle
But, I mean, we have
to look at this

20:47.806 --> 20:49.936 align:middle
as a-as a different situation.

20:49.936 --> 20:51.736 align:middle
There's, there are
differences and similarities.

20:51.736 --> 20:53.546 align:middle
I mean, it's a different time.

20:53.546 --> 20:56.816 align:middle
You know, we haven't had
real price stability fully

20:56.816 --> 20:59.046 align:middle
reestablished yet, and we
have to keep that in mind.

20:59.406 --> 21:02.466 align:middle
And, you know, we
also have-we hear

21:02.466 --> 21:06.276 align:middle
that people are very reluctant
to take on-to, you know, to,

21:06.316 --> 21:07.716 align:middle
to allow prices to go up.

21:07.716 --> 21:09.716 align:middle
At the same time, we hear
that businesses are intending

21:09.716 --> 21:11.196 align:middle
to pass many of these
prices through.

21:11.196 --> 21:12.806 align:middle
So it's hard to say how
this is going to work out.

21:14.446 --> 21:15.116 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

21:16.956 --> 21:18.296 align:middle
Steve. STEVE LIESMAN.

21:18.296 --> 21:19.236 align:middle
Steve, Steve Liesman, CNBC.

21:19.236 --> 21:20.676 align:middle
Thanks for taking my
question, Mr. Chairman.

21:20.676 --> 21:23.996 align:middle
The Bank of Canada in its
last policy statement said,

21:24.446 --> 21:27.586 align:middle
"Monetary policy cannot offset
the impacts of a trade war.

21:28.106 --> 21:29.616 align:middle
What it can and must
do is ensure

21:29.616 --> 21:32.296 align:middle
that higher prices do not
lead to ongoing inflation."

21:32.936 --> 21:36.236 align:middle
I wonder if that kind of
reflects your own sense

21:36.236 --> 21:38.676 align:middle
of prioritization,
faced with higher prices

21:39.006 --> 21:40.806 align:middle
and weaker growth-that
the idea is,

21:40.806 --> 21:41.956 align:middle
you have to take
care of inflation.

21:42.506 --> 21:43.146 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

21:43.436 --> 21:45.476 align:middle
You know-so we, we
have two goals, right?

21:45.476 --> 21:47.286 align:middle
We have maximum employment
and price stability,

21:47.416 --> 21:48.686 align:middle
and we have to balance those.

21:48.686 --> 21:51.276 align:middle
And, you know, there
can be situations

21:51.276 --> 21:52.976 align:middle
in which they are
in tension, right?

21:52.976 --> 21:54.376 align:middle
And we have, actually
have a provision

21:54.376 --> 21:56.926 align:middle
in our-in our consensus
statement

21:56.986 --> 21:58.376 align:middle
that says what we
should do in that case.

21:58.376 --> 22:01.956 align:middle
That's a very challenging,
you know, situation for,

22:01.956 --> 22:03.786 align:middle
for any central bank,
and certainly for us.

22:04.256 --> 22:06.116 align:middle
And so, what we say
that we'll do is, we'll,

22:06.396 --> 22:09.946 align:middle
we'll look how far each
of those two goals is-each

22:09.946 --> 22:11.886 align:middle
of those two measures
is from its goal,

22:12.516 --> 22:16.236 align:middle
and then we'll ask how long we
think it might take to get back

22:16.236 --> 22:18.126 align:middle
to the goal for each of them,
and we'll make a judgment,

22:18.126 --> 22:20.616 align:middle
because our, you know, our
tools work in one direction.

22:20.616 --> 22:22.256 align:middle
We're either tightening
or loosening.

22:22.766 --> 22:24.206 align:middle
So it's a very challenging
situation.

22:24.206 --> 22:26.606 align:middle
Let me say, we don't have
that situation right now.

22:26.606 --> 22:29.466 align:middle
That's not what the-that's not
where the economy is at all.

22:29.756 --> 22:31.466 align:middle
It's also not where
the forecast is.

22:31.466 --> 22:33.226 align:middle
I don't know any
mainstream forecast

22:33.376 --> 22:35.176 align:middle
that really shows
significant problems

22:35.176 --> 22:37.536 align:middle
like that, so- STEVE LIESMAN.

22:38.076 --> 22:39.136 align:middle
Well, just to follow up,

22:39.136 --> 22:43.566 align:middle
yesterday the UCLA Anderson
Forecast said there's-a high

22:43.616 --> 22:44.816 align:middle
probability of a recession.

22:44.816 --> 22:46.416 align:middle
Where, where do you
stand on whether

22:46.416 --> 22:48.266 align:middle
or not the slowdown you're
seeing creates a higher

22:48.266 --> 22:50.666 align:middle
probability or concern that
you may have on recession?

22:50.696 --> 22:51.016 align:middle
Thank you.

22:51.206 --> 22:51.656 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:51.656 --> 22:53.596 align:middle
You know, there's
always an unconditional

22:53.596 --> 22:56.386 align:middle
probability-possibility
of, of a recession.

22:56.966 --> 23:00.756 align:middle
It might be broadly in the range
of one in four at any time,

23:00.966 --> 23:02.456 align:middle
if you look back through,
through the years.

23:02.596 --> 23:04.046 align:middle
It-there could be,
within 12 months,

23:04.046 --> 23:05.476 align:middle
a one in four chance
of a recession.

23:05.706 --> 23:09.786 align:middle
So the question is, is whether
that-whether this current

23:09.966 --> 23:12.596 align:middle
situation, those
possibilities are elevated.

23:13.056 --> 23:15.996 align:middle
I will say this: We don't-we
don't make such a forecast.

23:15.996 --> 23:18.376 align:middle
If you look at outside
forecasts, forecasters have,

23:18.576 --> 23:20.116 align:middle
have generally raised-a number

23:20.116 --> 23:22.326 align:middle
of them have raised
their possibility

23:22.326 --> 23:25.406 align:middle
of a-of a recession somewhat,

23:25.656 --> 23:27.996 align:middle
but still at relatively
moderate levels-you know,

23:27.996 --> 23:30.006 align:middle
still in the region
of, of the traditional,

23:30.006 --> 23:31.206 align:middle
because they were extremely low.

23:31.206 --> 23:34.076 align:middle
If you go back two months,
people were saying that the,

23:34.146 --> 23:36.216 align:middle
the likelihood of a
recession was extremely low.

23:36.216 --> 23:38.156 align:middle
So, has moved up,
but it's not high.

23:39.696 --> 23:40.046 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

23:40.296 --> 23:43.126 align:middle
Chris. CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

23:43.856 --> 23:44.936 align:middle
Hi. Thank you.

23:44.936 --> 23:46.496 align:middle
Chris Rugaber at
Associated Press.

23:46.496 --> 23:51.116 align:middle
As you know, I guess, last night
the-President Trump fired two

23:51.116 --> 23:52.886 align:middle
members of the Federal
Trade Commission,

23:52.886 --> 23:57.146 align:middle
an independent agency, and
this could cause the kind

23:57.146 --> 23:59.396 align:middle
of legal fight about the
Administration's power

23:59.396 --> 24:00.786 align:middle
to fire independent people.

24:00.786 --> 24:03.546 align:middle
If those firings
stand, is that a threat

24:03.546 --> 24:04.776 align:middle
to the Fed's independence?

24:05.246 --> 24:07.566 align:middle
Could he do the same
thing to the Fed Board?

24:07.876 --> 24:08.986 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:08.986 --> 24:11.016 align:middle
So I think I, I did
answer that question

24:11.016 --> 24:14.616 align:middle
in this very room some time
ago, and I, I [have] no desire

24:14.616 --> 24:16.716 align:middle
to change that answer
and have nothing new

24:16.766 --> 24:17.686 align:middle
for you on that today.

24:17.906 --> 24:19.056 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

24:19.056 --> 24:23.356 align:middle
Well, I just-okay, well, maybe
I'll have another mulligan.

24:24.836 --> 24:26.796 align:middle
[Laughter] As you know,
I, I wanted to go back

24:26.796 --> 24:28.146 align:middle
to the consumer sentiment,

24:28.476 --> 24:30.176 align:middle
particularly the
inflation expectations

24:30.176 --> 24:31.836 align:middle
in the University
of Michigan survey.

24:32.196 --> 24:34.716 align:middle
In the summer of 2022,
you cited the rise

24:35.306 --> 24:37.966 align:middle
in the long-term inflation
expectations in that index

24:38.436 --> 24:41.726 align:middle
as the reason that you went big
with a three-quarter-point hike.

24:42.096 --> 24:43.146 align:middle
So I know you've-I
mean, you've talked

24:43.146 --> 24:45.426 align:middle
about all the different
measures now, but you seem

24:45.426 --> 24:47.696 align:middle
to not be placing the
same weight on that.

24:47.696 --> 24:50.676 align:middle
And so I'm just wondering,
are you dismissing that,

24:51.156 --> 24:53.056 align:middle
what we saw last week from
the University of Michigan,

24:53.056 --> 24:55.926 align:middle
or does that carry the same
weight as it did in the past?

24:56.526 --> 24:56.896 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:56.946 --> 24:58.306 align:middle
So I, I mentioned it back then,

24:58.306 --> 25:00.846 align:middle
but in no way did I place
a huge weight on it.

25:00.846 --> 25:04.866 align:middle
I think it, it-that
was an ex post story,

25:04.866 --> 25:05.646 align:middle
but it wasn't the case.

25:05.646 --> 25:07.566 align:middle
That was a preliminary
reading, and so is this.

25:08.026 --> 25:10.106 align:middle
And it's also-this
is-that, that Michigan,

25:10.106 --> 25:12.786 align:middle
the one you're referring to,
the longer-term thing-you know,

25:12.786 --> 25:15.306 align:middle
we look at it, and we don't
dismiss data that we don't like.

25:15.306 --> 25:17.666 align:middle
We, we force ourselves
to look at it.

25:17.666 --> 25:21.296 align:middle
But it is an outlier compared
to market-based and compared

25:21.296 --> 25:24.416 align:middle
to other survey-based
assessments

25:24.416 --> 25:27.266 align:middle
of longer-run inflation
expectations,

25:27.266 --> 25:28.476 align:middle
so we've got to keep
that in mind.

25:28.666 --> 25:31.956 align:middle
And I, I-again, I,
I would just say,

25:31.956 --> 25:33.876 align:middle
we look at-we look
at all of them.

25:33.876 --> 25:36.366 align:middle
And that one-that one's kind
of an outlier, but, you know,

25:36.716 --> 25:38.366 align:middle
nonetheless, we, we
take notice of it.

25:39.706 --> 25:40.866 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

25:41.146 --> 25:41.436 align:middle
Thank you.

25:41.616 --> 25:42.976 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

25:42.976 --> 25:44.116 align:middle
Michael. MICHAEL MCKEE.

25:44.116 --> 25:46.606 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Radio and Television.

25:46.606 --> 25:49.556 align:middle
There is a worry on Wall Street
that when you say you want

25:49.626 --> 25:53.846 align:middle
to study the net effect
of the fiscal policies,

25:53.846 --> 25:56.426 align:middle
we may see on the
economy that you would end

25:56.426 --> 25:59.326 align:middle
up waiting too long and
be "behind the curve"

25:59.326 --> 26:01.666 align:middle
in responding to any downturn.

26:01.666 --> 26:03.546 align:middle
How can you reassure people

26:03.546 --> 26:07.746 align:middle
that you can spot a
problem early enough,

26:07.746 --> 26:10.026 align:middle
unless you decide
to be preemptive?

26:11.406 --> 26:11.886 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:11.886 --> 26:15.036 align:middle
Yeah, look, we're, we're
aware of the-we're well aware

26:15.036 --> 26:16.946 align:middle
of how things are going to
evolve and the time frames

26:16.946 --> 26:19.196 align:middle
and all that, and, you know,
we will-we will use our tools

26:19.196 --> 26:23.036 align:middle
to foster achievement of our
goals to the-to the best we can.

26:23.036 --> 26:25.246 align:middle
And, of course, we're going
to try to be timely with that.

26:25.826 --> 26:28.786 align:middle
For right now, the hard
data are pretty solid.

26:29.146 --> 26:32.546 align:middle
We, we are obviously aware
of the soft sentiment data

26:32.546 --> 26:35.886 align:middle
and high uncertainty, and
we're watching that carefully.

26:35.966 --> 26:38.486 align:middle
And we think it's a, a
good time for us to wait

26:38.486 --> 26:41.206 align:middle
for further clarity before
we consider adjusting our,

26:41.356 --> 26:42.236 align:middle
our policy stance.

26:42.236 --> 26:42.536 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

26:42.536 --> 26:45.416 align:middle
Do you think it's going to
be hard to get clarity in a

26:45.526 --> 26:46.716 align:middle
"government by tweet"?

26:46.716 --> 26:48.306 align:middle
I mean, do you have a, a feeling

26:48.306 --> 26:51.126 align:middle
that at some point you actually
will have a forecast you

26:51.126 --> 26:51.626 align:middle
can trust?

26:51.626 --> 26:52.526 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:52.726 --> 26:53.486 align:middle
Yes, I think we will.

26:53.616 --> 26:55.906 align:middle
I just-it's hard to
say when that will be.

26:56.876 --> 26:59.826 align:middle
You know, we're-these
decisions are going to be made,

26:59.996 --> 27:00.536 align:middle
and they're going

27:00.536 --> 27:01.816 align:middle
to be implemented,
and then we'll know.

27:01.816 --> 27:03.876 align:middle
At that point, we'll know
what the decisions are,

27:03.876 --> 27:05.686 align:middle
and we'll have to make
assessments then about their,

27:05.686 --> 27:07.306 align:middle
their implications
for the economy.

27:07.696 --> 27:08.706 align:middle
Those things will happen.

27:08.706 --> 27:10.416 align:middle
A lot of them will happen
over the course of, you know,

27:10.416 --> 27:12.666 align:middle
in coming months, certainly
over the course of this year,

27:13.256 --> 27:15.006 align:middle
and we'll be adapting as we go.

27:15.406 --> 27:15.696 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

27:16.016 --> 27:17.256 align:middle
Rachel. RACHEL SIEGEL.

27:20.086 --> 27:20.706 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

27:20.766 --> 27:22.236 align:middle
Rachel Siegel from
the Washington Post.

27:22.316 --> 27:23.766 align:middle
Thank you for taking
our questions.

27:24.416 --> 27:25.606 align:middle
At the beginning,
you were talking

27:25.606 --> 27:29.056 align:middle
about separating the signal from
the noise, and tariff inflation

27:29.056 --> 27:30.186 align:middle
from nontariff inflation.

27:30.666 --> 27:32.136 align:middle
Can you "walk us
through" what that looked

27:32.136 --> 27:34.706 align:middle
like over the last couple of
months-if there were specifics,

27:34.736 --> 27:36.366 align:middle
from the January meeting to now,

27:36.366 --> 27:38.176 align:middle
that helped you make
those distinctions?

27:38.206 --> 27:38.416 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

27:38.416 --> 27:40.666 align:middle
So when we say "separating
the signal from the noise,"

27:40.756 --> 27:43.486 align:middle
that's just a way of saying
that things are highly uncertain

27:43.956 --> 27:45.716 align:middle
and that, you know,
you're reading

27:45.716 --> 27:48.156 align:middle
about developments-the news
is full of developments

27:48.156 --> 27:50.646 align:middle
of tariffs being put on and
taken off and things like that.

27:51.126 --> 27:53.836 align:middle
That's-some of that is noise
in the sense that it's,

27:53.836 --> 27:55.946 align:middle
it's not really telling
you anything.

27:56.346 --> 27:58.216 align:middle
You're trying to extract
a signal from that,

27:58.376 --> 28:00.766 align:middle
and the signal is what's
going to be the effect

28:00.846 --> 28:02.926 align:middle
on economic activity,
on inflation,

28:02.926 --> 28:04.306 align:middle
on employment, and
all those things.

28:04.306 --> 28:06.906 align:middle
So, so that's really when
we say "signal and noise."

28:06.906 --> 28:08.016 align:middle
Sorry, the second thing was-?

28:08.436 --> 28:08.906 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

28:08.906 --> 28:11.516 align:middle
Or, similarly for tariff
inflation, nontariff inflation,

28:11.516 --> 28:12.576 align:middle
ways that you're
making the distinction.

28:12.576 --> 28:12.946 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:12.946 --> 28:14.876 align:middle
That's, that's sort of a,
a special case of that-of,

28:14.876 --> 28:17.966 align:middle
you know, with the idea
being-and I think-do think

28:17.966 --> 28:20.156 align:middle
that the first two months of
this year are a great example.

28:20.156 --> 28:23.776 align:middle
You've got high readings for
goods inflation after a string

28:24.296 --> 28:27.206 align:middle
of readings that averaged
close to zero, and you have

28:27.206 --> 28:29.776 align:middle
to ask-it's coming during
tariffs, but, you know,

28:29.776 --> 28:33.776 align:middle
you-it's very hard to, actually,
scientifically go back and match

28:33.776 --> 28:35.416 align:middle
up those increases
and say, "Yes,

28:35.666 --> 28:37.426 align:middle
I can prove that
that's from tariffs."

28:37.456 --> 28:39.086 align:middle
But it kind of has to
be, to some extent.

28:39.486 --> 28:40.156 align:middle
Plus, noise.

28:40.156 --> 28:43.656 align:middle
There can be idiosyncratic
readings in various categories,

28:43.656 --> 28:45.786 align:middle
which will shortly reverse,
and that, that happens, too,

28:45.786 --> 28:47.446 align:middle
and that could be
a big piece of it.

28:47.446 --> 28:48.296 align:middle
You know, I think we'll know.

28:48.296 --> 28:48.996 align:middle
In a couple of months,

28:48.996 --> 28:50.966 align:middle
we'll know whether
those were-you know,

28:50.966 --> 28:51.886 align:middle
where that really was from.

28:51.886 --> 28:54.346 align:middle
But it's a-that's another
case where I think it's going

28:54.346 --> 28:59.526 align:middle
to be very, very challenging to
unpack the inflation that we see

28:59.616 --> 29:02.076 align:middle
over the course of this
year and be able to say

29:02.076 --> 29:05.246 align:middle
with confidence how much of that
came from inflation and how much

29:05.246 --> 29:07.986 align:middle
of it-sorry, from tariffs-and
how much of it didn't.

29:08.326 --> 29:09.476 align:middle
But that's what we'll be doing.

29:09.476 --> 29:11.086 align:middle
We'll be doing that, and
so will everybody else,

29:11.086 --> 29:13.376 align:middle
and we'll all be
trying very hard to,

29:13.796 --> 29:14.736 align:middle
to make that assessment.

29:14.736 --> 29:16.936 align:middle
And, you know, I, I'm
sure we will make a lot

29:16.936 --> 29:17.646 align:middle
of progress on that.

29:17.646 --> 29:18.356 align:middle
And we already have.

29:18.496 --> 29:19.896 align:middle
But it, it's going
to be a challenge.

29:19.896 --> 29:20.306 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

29:20.306 --> 29:22.526 align:middle
Do you have a sense yet
as to what, in your mind,

29:22.526 --> 29:25.346 align:middle
what makes something cross
from noise to a signal-what

29:25.346 --> 29:26.316 align:middle
that threshold would look like?

29:26.316 --> 29:27.226 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:27.226 --> 29:28.696 align:middle
You know, it would depend
on what we're talking about.

29:28.906 --> 29:30.866 align:middle
I mean, obviously,
you're looking

29:30.866 --> 29:34.466 align:middle
for direct evidence that,
that particular pieces of,

29:34.466 --> 29:38.626 align:middle
of inflation are, are-or are
clearly not-caused by tariffs.

29:38.626 --> 29:41.956 align:middle
For example, something that was,
you know, in the service sector

29:41.956 --> 29:44.416 align:middle
that was far away from
anything that's tariffed.

29:44.416 --> 29:47.176 align:middle
You, you might think, okay,
well that is-like, frankly,

29:47.176 --> 29:50.146 align:middle
housing services
inflation-which, which,

29:50.146 --> 29:51.586 align:middle
by the way, has been
behaving well.

29:52.006 --> 29:55.826 align:middle
You know-which, for, for some
time, was kind of our problem.

29:55.856 --> 29:59.216 align:middle
Now it's been-it's slow, but
it's definitely, you know,

29:59.326 --> 30:00.906 align:middle
moving down in a-in
a very good way.

30:01.716 --> 30:04.236 align:middle
It's more now with goods
and, to some extent, with,

30:04.636 --> 30:06.396 align:middle
with nonhousing services
inflation.

30:07.606 --> 30:08.266 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

30:08.416 --> 30:09.786 align:middle
Kelly. KELLY O'GRADY.

30:10.276 --> 30:13.216 align:middle
Thanks for taking our
questions, Chair Powell.

30:13.216 --> 30:14.906 align:middle
Kelly O'Grady, CBS News.

30:15.006 --> 30:18.146 align:middle
So consumer sentiment
has dipped dramatically,

30:18.226 --> 30:21.946 align:middle
but you say the economy and
the hard data is still solid.

30:22.456 --> 30:26.006 align:middle
What is your message to
consumers that clearly disagree

30:26.006 --> 30:28.076 align:middle
and don't feel that strength?

30:28.076 --> 30:29.536 align:middle
Because the hard
data they're looking

30:29.646 --> 30:30.586 align:middle
at is their grocery bill.

30:31.526 --> 30:31.876 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:31.876 --> 30:32.936 align:middle
Okay, so a couple things.

30:32.936 --> 30:36.486 align:middle
The grocery bill is about,
about past inflation, really.

30:36.606 --> 30:39.956 align:middle
There was inflation in '21, '22,
and '23, and prices went up.

30:40.276 --> 30:42.806 align:middle
The current level-it's
not the change in prices.

30:42.806 --> 30:45.866 align:middle
It's, they're unhappy-and,
and they're not wrong

30:45.866 --> 30:47.946 align:middle
to be unhappy-that prices
went up quite a bit,

30:48.386 --> 30:49.886 align:middle
and they're paying a
lot for those things.

30:50.346 --> 30:52.846 align:middle
So that's-I think that is the
fundamental fact and has been

30:52.846 --> 30:56.126 align:middle
for a long time-a couple
years-why people are unhappy

30:56.126 --> 30:58.386 align:middle
with the-with the economy.

30:58.386 --> 30:59.946 align:middle
It's not that the
economy's not growing.

30:59.946 --> 31:01.456 align:middle
It's not that inflation's
really high.

31:01.456 --> 31:02.866 align:middle
It's not that unemployment
is high.

31:02.996 --> 31:03.926 align:middle
It's none of those things.

31:04.336 --> 31:07.406 align:middle
We have, you know, 4.1
percent unemployment.

31:07.406 --> 31:09.896 align:middle
We've got 2 percent
growth, and, you know, it's,

31:09.896 --> 31:10.986 align:middle
it's a pretty good economy.

31:11.146 --> 31:13.896 align:middle
But people are unhappy
because of the price level.

31:13.896 --> 31:16.256 align:middle
And, and I do-we completely
understand and accept that.

31:16.256 --> 31:16.946 align:middle
KELLY O'GRADY.

31:17.186 --> 31:20.716 align:middle
And just to follow up: Why are
you still projecting two rate

31:20.716 --> 31:24.236 align:middle
cuts this year if your own
projections show inflation

31:24.236 --> 31:25.406 align:middle
higher for longer?

31:25.736 --> 31:27.416 align:middle
Does that mean you
see a slowdown

31:27.416 --> 31:29.066 align:middle
in economic growth
as a real threat?

31:29.676 --> 31:30.256 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:30.256 --> 31:33.306 align:middle
So I, I think if
you-yeah, I mean, remember,

31:33.306 --> 31:36.356 align:middle
we came into this with-at
the December meeting,

31:36.586 --> 31:39.886 align:middle
and the median was two
cuts, the median was.

31:40.426 --> 31:42.966 align:middle
And so you come in, and
you see, broadly speaking,

31:43.696 --> 31:45.636 align:middle
weaker growth but
higher inflation.

31:46.246 --> 31:49.026 align:middle
And they kind of balance each
other out, so you think-and,

31:49.026 --> 31:51.026 align:middle
and unemployment is
really, really only,

31:51.026 --> 31:52.366 align:middle
only a one-tenth change.

31:52.616 --> 31:55.046 align:middle
So it's-there's just not a
big change in the forecast.

31:55.046 --> 31:55.726 align:middle
There really isn't.

31:56.266 --> 32:00.156 align:middle
Modest, you know,
meaningfully higher growth

32:00.876 --> 32:02.286 align:middle
and meaningfully
higher inflation,

32:02.286 --> 32:03.996 align:middle
which call for different
responses, right?

32:04.076 --> 32:07.246 align:middle
So they cancel each other out,
and people just said, "Okay,

32:07.496 --> 32:08.456 align:middle
I'm going to stay here."

32:08.506 --> 32:11.026 align:middle
But the second factor is,
it's so highly uncertain.

32:11.636 --> 32:14.036 align:middle
Is just-you know, we're
sitting here thinking-and we,

32:14.206 --> 32:17.526 align:middle
we obviously are in, in
touch with businesses

32:17.526 --> 32:19.196 align:middle
and households all
over the country.

32:19.196 --> 32:22.596 align:middle
We have an extraordinary
network of contacts that come

32:22.596 --> 32:24.896 align:middle
in through the Reserve
Banks and put it

32:24.896 --> 32:27.246 align:middle
in the Beige Book-and also
through contacts at the Board.

32:27.916 --> 32:30.076 align:middle
And we get all that,
and we, we do understand

32:30.076 --> 32:32.536 align:middle
that sentiment has
fallen off pretty sharply,

32:33.036 --> 32:34.996 align:middle
but economic activity
has not yet.

32:35.186 --> 32:36.966 align:middle
And so we're watching carefully.

32:37.266 --> 32:40.116 align:middle
So I would tell people
that the economy seems

32:40.116 --> 32:41.756 align:middle
to be-seems to be healthy.

32:42.026 --> 32:45.556 align:middle
We understand that, that
sentiment is, is quite negative

32:45.656 --> 32:49.206 align:middle
at this time, and that probably
has to do with, you know,

32:49.516 --> 32:51.606 align:middle
turmoil at the beginning
of an Administration.

32:51.606 --> 32:55.996 align:middle
It's making, you know, big
changes in, in areas of policy,

32:56.566 --> 32:58.616 align:middle
and that's probably part of it.

32:58.776 --> 33:00.876 align:middle
I do think the underlying
unhappiness people have

33:00.876 --> 33:02.846 align:middle
about the economy,
though, is more-is more

33:02.846 --> 33:03.586 align:middle
about the price level.

33:05.636 --> 33:05.926 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

33:06.076 --> 33:09.376 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

33:09.476 --> 33:10.956 align:middle
Hi. Victoria Guida
with Politico.

33:11.006 --> 33:14.816 align:middle
I wanted to ask, first of
all, if you could clarify.

33:14.916 --> 33:18.606 align:middle
You were talking about how
tariffs were a good part

33:18.606 --> 33:20.976 align:middle
of the uptick in the
inflation forecasts,

33:20.976 --> 33:23.836 align:middle
and I was just wondering what
that specifically refers to.

33:23.836 --> 33:26.496 align:middle
Is that the tariffs that have
already been put in place?

33:26.496 --> 33:28.046 align:middle
Is that anticipating
some of the tariffs

33:28.086 --> 33:29.956 align:middle
that might be coming on April 2?

33:29.956 --> 33:33.956 align:middle
And then also, if you wouldn't
mind talking a little more

33:33.956 --> 33:36.666 align:middle
about the balance sheet
decision and what drove that.

33:36.666 --> 33:39.556 align:middle
Did that have anything
to do with expectations

33:39.556 --> 33:41.886 align:middle
of how the debt ceiling-raising
the debt ceiling might affect

33:41.886 --> 33:42.516 align:middle
the reserve supply?

33:42.856 --> 33:43.076 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:43.076 --> 33:45.146 align:middle
Yeah, so, in the SEP, you'll see

33:45.146 --> 33:47.106 align:middle
that there's not
further progress

33:47.576 --> 33:49.036 align:middle
on core inflation this year.

33:49.036 --> 33:51.756 align:middle
We're kind of side
flatlining, going sideways.

33:52.496 --> 33:55.196 align:middle
We don't ask people to say-to
write down how much of this is

33:55.196 --> 33:56.606 align:middle
from tariffs and how
much of it is not.

33:56.866 --> 33:58.036 align:middle
But some of it is from tariffs.

33:58.356 --> 33:59.746 align:middle
We know that tariffs are coming,

33:59.746 --> 34:02.286 align:middle
and we know that they're
probably already-all,

34:02.286 --> 34:06.596 align:middle
all forecasters have tariff
inflation affecting core PCE

34:06.596 --> 34:08.736 align:middle
inflation [and] core
CPI inflation this year,

34:09.146 --> 34:09.836 align:middle
without exception.

34:09.836 --> 34:10.896 align:middle
I'm not aware of an exception.

34:10.896 --> 34:12.086 align:middle
So it's in there.

34:12.186 --> 34:13.616 align:middle
I can't tell you how
much of that it is.

34:14.376 --> 34:16.626 align:middle
In terms of the balance
sheet-so, yeah,

34:16.626 --> 34:22.786 align:middle
we-I think-I guess the way
I'd say it is, you know,

34:22.786 --> 34:25.036 align:middle
it was the flows
in and out of the,

34:25.636 --> 34:29.716 align:middle
the TGA that got us thinking
about it, but as we, you know,

34:29.716 --> 34:32.366 align:middle
as we thought about it,
we really came to the view

34:33.086 --> 34:36.676 align:middle
that this was a good time to,
to make the move that we made.

34:37.426 --> 34:43.126 align:middle
And, broadly, Committee came
around-came around to the view

34:43.126 --> 34:45.396 align:middle
that we, we would do the
same thing we'd already done,

34:45.396 --> 34:50.316 align:middle
which is, once we-I guess
in June, we-was it June?

34:50.316 --> 34:54.256 align:middle
Whenever it was, we, we
lowered the pace of QT,

34:54.256 --> 34:55.786 align:middle
and we're just going
to do that again.

34:55.786 --> 34:57.006 align:middle
We're going to cut
it roughly in half.

34:57.586 --> 35:00.446 align:middle
And the sense of that is,
if you're cutting the pace

35:00.446 --> 35:03.866 align:middle
of QT roughly in half, then
the runway is probably doubled,

35:04.256 --> 35:04.586 align:middle
okay?

35:04.586 --> 35:07.976 align:middle
So it's going-it's going
to be slower for longer.

35:08.426 --> 35:09.876 align:middle
And people really liked that.

35:09.926 --> 35:11.286 align:middle
People thought, "That's,
that's a good idea."

35:11.286 --> 35:12.336 align:middle
You know, it's like a plane.

35:12.336 --> 35:14.526 align:middle
You can think of it like a
plane coming in for a landing.

35:15.156 --> 35:17.906 align:middle
As we get closer and we-by
the way, we still think

35:17.906 --> 35:21.786 align:middle
that reserves are abundant,
although you begin to see some

35:21.786 --> 35:23.576 align:middle
of the-some of the
things we look at begin

35:23.576 --> 35:25.136 align:middle
to react a little bit,
but we still think

35:25.136 --> 35:25.736 align:middle
that they're abundant.

35:25.736 --> 35:29.056 align:middle
And, of course, now, the TGA is
emptying out, so reserves are,

35:29.206 --> 35:31.456 align:middle
are higher now, so you
can't really see the

35:31.456 --> 35:32.236 align:middle
underlying signal.

35:32.886 --> 35:35.986 align:middle
So we came around to the
view, and, and it had a lot

35:35.986 --> 35:37.166 align:middle
of appeal, and so we did it.

35:37.166 --> 35:40.586 align:middle
It really-it has no implications
at all for monetary policy.

35:41.156 --> 35:42.916 align:middle
It has no implications at all

35:42.946 --> 35:44.746 align:middle
for the ultimate size
of the balance sheet.

35:45.046 --> 35:47.496 align:middle
It isn't sending a
signal in any hidden way

35:48.086 --> 35:49.876 align:middle
that you can try to tease out.

35:50.156 --> 35:50.896 align:middle
It's just not there.

35:50.896 --> 35:54.016 align:middle
We're, we're basically-it's
very consistent with our plans

35:54.226 --> 35:56.516 align:middle
and our practices
that we've published

35:56.516 --> 35:59.476 align:middle
and that we've followed since
we began this-what is a very

35:59.476 --> 36:03.006 align:middle
successful, you know,
rundown of the balance sheet.

36:03.516 --> 36:06.606 align:middle
Again, [this is] the second
time that we've slowed the pace.

36:07.206 --> 36:09.826 align:middle
And we've said that we would
stop when we were somewhat

36:09.826 --> 36:11.916 align:middle
above the level [of
reserves] we judge as ample.

36:11.916 --> 36:13.866 align:middle
And, clearly, we're not at
that level yet, but we're going

36:13.866 --> 36:15.206 align:middle
to be approaching
it more slowly.

36:15.616 --> 36:17.826 align:middle
It's a commonsense kind
of a-kind of a thing,

36:17.826 --> 36:20.016 align:middle
and it had-it had pretty
broad appeal, I will say.

36:21.926 --> 36:23.046 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

36:23.826 --> 36:24.986 align:middle
Claire. CLAIRE JONES.

36:25.296 --> 36:26.886 align:middle
Claire Jones, Financial Times.

36:28.066 --> 36:29.906 align:middle
You said in January,
Chair Powell,

36:30.276 --> 36:34.006 align:middle
that inflation expectations
remained well anchored.

36:34.506 --> 36:37.906 align:middle
Would you still say they
remain well anchored today?

36:37.906 --> 36:41.236 align:middle
And just to reiterate the
question others have asked,

36:41.236 --> 36:42.936 align:middle
if they're not so well anchored,

36:43.396 --> 36:45.276 align:middle
why hasn't there been
a more radical shift

36:45.336 --> 36:47.066 align:middle
to the policy path today?

36:47.396 --> 36:47.686 align:middle
Thank you.

36:48.006 --> 36:48.706 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:48.776 --> 36:49.966 align:middle
So when we-when we talk

36:49.966 --> 36:53.386 align:middle
about inflation expectations
being well anchored,

36:53.926 --> 36:56.926 align:middle
we're talking about longer-run
inflation expectations.

36:57.586 --> 37:02.006 align:middle
And they really haven't moved
much, I mean, if at all.

37:02.006 --> 37:04.756 align:middle
There, there's one reading
that everyone's focusing

37:04.756 --> 37:07.256 align:middle
on that's higher, but
the other survey readings

37:07.256 --> 37:10.976 align:middle
and the market-based readings
all show relatively, you know,

37:11.166 --> 37:13.336 align:middle
well-anchored inflation
expectations.

37:13.796 --> 37:17.226 align:middle
You would expect that
expectations of inflation

37:17.226 --> 37:19.476 align:middle
over the course of a
year would move around,

37:19.476 --> 37:21.346 align:middle
because conditions
change, and in this case,

37:21.846 --> 37:23.116 align:middle
we have tariffs coming in.

37:23.116 --> 37:24.676 align:middle
We don't know how
big, what's-there's

37:24.676 --> 37:26.016 align:middle
so many things we don't know,

37:26.016 --> 37:28.126 align:middle
but we kind of know there
are going to be tariffs,

37:28.236 --> 37:30.086 align:middle
and they tend to
bring growth down.

37:30.086 --> 37:33.326 align:middle
They tend to bring inflation up
at the-in the first instance.

37:33.326 --> 37:36.006 align:middle
So, so I would say,
you, you know,

37:36.006 --> 37:37.426 align:middle
I'm not dismissing
what we're seeing

37:37.426 --> 37:39.066 align:middle
in short-term inflation
expectations.

37:39.166 --> 37:41.676 align:middle
We, as I mentioned,
follow that very carefully.

37:42.156 --> 37:45.506 align:middle
But when we say expectations
are well anchored,

37:45.506 --> 37:48.216 align:middle
we're really looking at, you
know, longer term, five year

37:48.216 --> 37:51.526 align:middle
and out, and there's really
no story to tell five years

37:51.526 --> 37:54.616 align:middle
and out, either in market
based or, or in surveys.

37:55.156 --> 37:56.226 align:middle
But we keep-we'll watch it.

37:56.226 --> 37:58.966 align:middle
I mean, we're not, you
know, we're, we're not going

37:58.966 --> 38:01.676 align:middle
to miss any evidence
that longer-term

38:01.676 --> 38:05.366 align:middle
or medium-term inflation
expectations are, are moving.

38:05.766 --> 38:06.146 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

38:07.366 --> 38:07.686 align:middle
Neil. NEIL IRWIN.

38:10.266 --> 38:11.026 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

38:11.026 --> 38:12.396 align:middle
Neil Irwin with Axios.

38:12.816 --> 38:15.146 align:middle
Treasury Secretary Bessent has
observed that a large share

38:15.146 --> 38:17.386 align:middle
of job growth over the last
couple of years has been

38:17.386 --> 38:18.576 align:middle
in what he calls government

38:18.576 --> 38:20.866 align:middle
or government-adjacent
sectors-health care, education.

38:21.316 --> 38:22.926 align:middle
Do you agree that
there's some weakness

38:22.926 --> 38:25.016 align:middle
in underlying private
job growth,

38:25.016 --> 38:27.546 align:middle
and do you see the composition
of job growth as something

38:27.546 --> 38:28.786 align:middle
that has policy implications?

38:29.746 --> 38:30.076 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:30.076 --> 38:31.156 align:middle
So that, that has been the case.

38:31.156 --> 38:33.446 align:middle
We talked about that over
the course of the last year.

38:33.446 --> 38:36.966 align:middle
There were-there were some
good number of months and times

38:36.966 --> 38:41.336 align:middle
when a lot of the job creation
was, was concentrated in,

38:41.936 --> 38:44.286 align:middle
you know, educational
institutions, health care,

38:44.926 --> 38:46.866 align:middle
state governments,
things like that.

38:47.386 --> 38:48.656 align:middle
There were also times

38:48.656 --> 38:52.146 align:middle
when private-sector job
growth has been moving,

38:52.146 --> 38:54.626 align:middle
moving in a healthy frame-and
they're all-they're all jobs,

38:54.706 --> 38:56.736 align:middle
but-and remember,
we're, you know,

38:56.736 --> 38:59.486 align:middle
we're at very low
unemployment for, you know,

38:59.486 --> 39:00.826 align:middle
for, for quite a time now.

39:00.826 --> 39:03.706 align:middle
So I, I think it's a good
labor market, but it's-look,

39:03.706 --> 39:05.226 align:middle
it's something that
we monitor carefully.

39:05.666 --> 39:07.876 align:middle
From our standpoint,
employment is employment.

39:08.216 --> 39:12.056 align:middle
But I, you know-the, the
elected government is entitled

39:12.056 --> 39:14.306 align:middle
to have-you know, we,
we don't have policies

39:14.306 --> 39:16.286 align:middle
that address different
kinds of employment.

39:16.716 --> 39:19.386 align:middle
But, but the elected government
has a-has a different role,

39:19.386 --> 39:21.076 align:middle
and they can-they,
they can have those.

39:21.306 --> 39:21.596 align:middle
NEIL IRWIN.

39:21.596 --> 39:24.396 align:middle
True. Quick QT question:
Does the Committee envision

39:24.436 --> 39:26.896 align:middle
at some point tapering the
MBS runoff, runoff as well?

39:27.756 --> 39:28.476 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:28.476 --> 39:30.996 align:middle
I think tapering
it-I, I don't know.

39:30.996 --> 39:32.206 align:middle
There's no plan to do that.

39:32.726 --> 39:35.876 align:middle
You know, at a certain
point, we'll stop runoff.

39:35.936 --> 39:38.996 align:middle
And we may or may not stop
MBS runoff, though, because,

39:39.086 --> 39:41.176 align:middle
you know, we can-we can-we,

39:41.176 --> 39:43.716 align:middle
we want to stop runoff
in net at some point.

39:43.716 --> 39:45.966 align:middle
We haven't made any decisions
about that, but, you know, we,

39:45.966 --> 39:50.016 align:middle
we want the-we want the MBS
to roll off our balance sheet.

39:50.786 --> 39:52.716 align:middle
We really, strongly desire that,

39:52.816 --> 39:55.786 align:middle
so-but we haven't made any
decisions about that, you know.

39:55.786 --> 39:59.076 align:middle
We, we will-we-I think we'd,
we'd look carefully at letting

39:59.076 --> 40:01.436 align:middle
that keep going, but
hold the overall size

40:01.436 --> 40:04.866 align:middle
of the balance sheet
in, you know, constant,

40:04.866 --> 40:06.696 align:middle
at some point-a point
that we're not at yet.

40:06.696 --> 40:07.246 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

40:08.366 --> 40:10.916 align:middle
Simon. SIMON RABINOVITCH.

40:10.916 --> 40:12.416 align:middle
Simon Rabinovitch
with the Economist.

40:12.486 --> 40:13.336 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

40:13.736 --> 40:14.966 align:middle
Several times today, you've said

40:14.966 --> 40:16.666 align:middle
that you feel you're
well positioned

40:16.666 --> 40:18.326 align:middle
to wait for greater clarity.

40:18.726 --> 40:19.946 align:middle
At the same time,
you could point

40:19.946 --> 40:21.816 align:middle
to quite a few growth
risks at the moment.

40:21.816 --> 40:24.856 align:middle
We've seen a stock market
that's gone quite wobbly,

40:25.186 --> 40:28.576 align:middle
rapidly cooling housing sales,
plunging confidence surveys.

40:29.146 --> 40:31.516 align:middle
Today, not only did the SEP
mark down the growth outlook,

40:31.886 --> 40:35.006 align:middle
17 of 19 see risks
to the downside.

40:35.336 --> 40:37.536 align:middle
So my question is,
how confident are you

40:37.536 --> 40:38.526 align:middle
that you're well positioned?

40:38.526 --> 40:40.516 align:middle
Or is that one more thing
that you're uncertain about?

40:41.026 --> 40:41.936 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:41.936 --> 40:44.276 align:middle
I'm confident that we're
well positioned in the sense

40:44.276 --> 40:46.276 align:middle
that we're, we're well
positioned to move

40:46.356 --> 40:48.096 align:middle
in the direction
we'll need to move.

40:48.096 --> 40:50.506 align:middle
I, I mean, I, I don't
know anyone who has a lot

40:50.506 --> 40:51.856 align:middle
of confidence in their forecast.

40:52.216 --> 40:55.136 align:middle
I mean, the point is, we
are-we are at, you know,

40:55.136 --> 40:57.086 align:middle
we're at a place
where we can cut

40:57.176 --> 41:00.516 align:middle
or we-or we can hold what is
a clearly a restrictive stance

41:00.516 --> 41:01.156 align:middle
of policy.

41:01.726 --> 41:03.136 align:middle
And that's what I mean.

41:03.136 --> 41:05.176 align:middle
I, I mean, I think
we're-that's well positioned.

41:05.826 --> 41:07.736 align:middle
Forecasting right
now, it's-you know,

41:07.796 --> 41:10.016 align:middle
forecasting is always
very, very hard,

41:10.056 --> 41:14.106 align:middle
and in the current situation, I
just think it's-uncertainty is,

41:14.106 --> 41:15.496 align:middle
you know, remarkably high.

41:15.496 --> 41:16.636 align:middle
SIMON RABINOVITCH.

41:16.636 --> 41:19.326 align:middle
And, sorry, standing here today,
would you be surprised to,

41:19.496 --> 41:21.716 align:middle
to pivot back towards
rate cuts in May?

41:22.656 --> 41:23.186 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:23.186 --> 41:24.916 align:middle
I, like, I, I think
we're not going

41:24.916 --> 41:26.486 align:middle
to be in any hurry to move.

41:26.806 --> 41:30.916 align:middle
And, as I mentioned, I think
we're, we're well positioned

41:31.316 --> 41:34.656 align:middle
to wait for further clarity,
and not in any hurry.

41:36.706 --> 41:37.486 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

41:38.456 --> 41:39.816 align:middle
Matt Egan.

41:40.156 --> 41:40.726 align:middle
MATT EGAN.

41:40.766 --> 41:41.396 align:middle
Matt Egan with CNN.

41:41.586 --> 41:42.406 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

41:42.866 --> 41:46.326 align:middle
The Fed's statement released
today removed the line

41:46.366 --> 41:48.836 align:middle
that previously said "the
Committee judges that the risks

41:48.886 --> 41:50.456 align:middle
to achieving its employment

41:50.926 --> 41:53.666 align:middle
and inflation goals are
roughly in balance."

41:53.746 --> 41:55.606 align:middle
Can you explain the
decision to remove that line?

41:56.126 --> 41:59.046 align:middle
Does it mean that
you're now more concerned

41:59.046 --> 42:01.176 align:middle
about inflation or
about employment?

42:01.386 --> 42:01.806 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:01.806 --> 42:03.446 align:middle
Actually, it does not mean
either of those things.

42:03.876 --> 42:09.176 align:middle
You know, sometimes with
language they-it, it lives its,

42:09.176 --> 42:13.276 align:middle
its useful life, and then
we take it off, and we-and,

42:13.276 --> 42:14.166 align:middle
and that was the case there.

42:14.166 --> 42:16.436 align:middle
There's really not meant
to be any signal here.

42:16.966 --> 42:20.506 align:middle
Over the past year, you
know, conveying this sense

42:20.506 --> 42:23.376 align:middle
of the balance of risks
was important that they be

42:23.376 --> 42:25.116 align:middle
in balance or close
to being in balance.

42:25.576 --> 42:28.346 align:middle
That was useful as we approach
liftoff, if you remember.

42:28.736 --> 42:29.606 align:middle
But we're past that.

42:29.606 --> 42:32.526 align:middle
Or, I'm sorry, no-beginning,
beginning to cut.

42:32.686 --> 42:34.536 align:middle
So we just-so we
just took it out.

42:34.536 --> 42:38.116 align:middle
I, I actually would say that
the more important thing now

42:38.116 --> 42:41.436 align:middle
about risks-and this is
in, in pages like 10, 11,

42:41.436 --> 42:43.446 align:middle
12 of the SEP-if you look,

42:44.126 --> 42:49.556 align:middle
participants widely raised
their estimate of, of the risks

42:49.706 --> 42:53.266 align:middle
to our-of uncertainty, but
also of the risks to growth

42:54.266 --> 42:56.556 align:middle
in our employment and
inflation mandates.

42:56.976 --> 42:58.826 align:middle
That's, that's a more
salient point now

42:58.826 --> 43:00.946 align:middle
than whether they're-whether
they're in balance.

43:01.726 --> 43:01.976 align:middle
MATT EGAN.

43:02.126 --> 43:03.596 align:middle
Just on the, the stock market,

43:03.596 --> 43:05.966 align:middle
the stock market has obviously
declined significantly

43:05.966 --> 43:08.436 align:middle
since the Fed last met.

43:08.496 --> 43:10.976 align:middle
Are you concerned
at all about some

43:10.976 --> 43:14.606 align:middle
of the market volatility having
a real economic impact in terms

43:14.606 --> 43:17.416 align:middle
of hurting business spending
or consumer spending,

43:17.416 --> 43:19.206 align:middle
especially among
higher-income households?

43:19.206 --> 43:19.946 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

43:20.776 --> 43:23.846 align:middle
So financial conditions matter
to us because, you know,

43:23.846 --> 43:26.436 align:middle
financial conditions are the
main channel to the real economy

43:26.496 --> 43:28.356 align:middle
through which our
policy has its effect.

43:28.356 --> 43:29.466 align:middle
So, so they're important.

43:29.826 --> 43:32.826 align:middle
But what matters from,
from a Fed standpoint

43:32.826 --> 43:35.636 align:middle
for the macroeconomy
is material changes

43:35.636 --> 43:38.576 align:middle
to overall financial
conditions that are persistent,

43:39.066 --> 43:40.566 align:middle
that last for a while-long
enough

43:40.566 --> 43:43.276 align:middle
to actually affect
economic activity.

43:43.276 --> 43:44.656 align:middle
So that's what we're
looking for.

43:45.216 --> 43:49.296 align:middle
I, I'm not going to opine
on the appropriate level

43:49.576 --> 43:52.236 align:middle
of any market-equity,
debt, commodities,

43:52.236 --> 43:53.076 align:middle
or anything like that.

43:53.686 --> 43:56.586 align:middle
And I would just point you to
the-to the bigger picture again.

43:57.016 --> 44:00.326 align:middle
You know, the, the real economy,
the, the hard data are still in,

44:00.356 --> 44:01.306 align:middle
in reasonably good shape.

44:01.766 --> 44:05.076 align:middle
It's the soft data-it's the
surveys that are showing,

44:05.456 --> 44:07.986 align:middle
you know, significant
concerns, downside risks,

44:07.986 --> 44:08.846 align:middle
and those kind of things.

44:09.356 --> 44:10.246 align:middle
We don't dismiss that.

44:10.246 --> 44:12.166 align:middle
We're watching it
carefully, but, you know, we,

44:12.166 --> 44:13.656 align:middle
we don't want to
get ahead of that.

44:13.656 --> 44:16.356 align:middle
You know, we, we want to
focus on the hard data.

44:16.356 --> 44:19.736 align:middle
This-if it's-if that's going
to affect the hard data,

44:19.736 --> 44:22.496 align:middle
we should know it very quickly,
and, and, of course, we'll,

44:22.586 --> 44:23.336 align:middle
we'll understand that.

44:23.336 --> 44:24.446 align:middle
But you don't see that yet.

44:24.446 --> 44:24.846 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

44:25.626 --> 44:29.416 align:middle
Jennifer. JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

44:30.246 --> 44:31.076 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

44:31.076 --> 44:33.276 align:middle
Jennifer Schonberger
with Yahoo Finance.

44:33.356 --> 44:36.856 align:middle
As you look to navigate higher
inflation and lower growth,

44:36.856 --> 44:40.296 align:middle
the Fed has talked about heeding
the lessons from the 1970s.

44:40.806 --> 44:43.056 align:middle
Is the Fed willing
to have a recession

44:43.056 --> 44:45.736 align:middle
if it means breaking
the back of inflation?

44:46.816 --> 44:47.396 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:47.396 --> 44:51.956 align:middle
Well, fortunately,
we're in a situation

44:51.956 --> 44:56.216 align:middle
where we have seen inflation
move down from, you know,

44:56.216 --> 44:58.646 align:middle
higher levels to pretty
close to 2 percent,

44:59.356 --> 45:03.296 align:middle
while the unemployment rate
has remained very consistent

45:03.296 --> 45:04.996 align:middle
with full employment,
4.1 percent.

45:05.166 --> 45:08.646 align:middle
So we now have inflation coming
in from an exogenous source,

45:08.706 --> 45:12.056 align:middle
but the underlying inflationary
picture before that was,

45:12.056 --> 45:14.286 align:middle
you know, basically 2½
percent inflation, I would say,

45:14.796 --> 45:18.486 align:middle
and 2 percent growth, and
4 percent unemployment.

45:18.486 --> 45:20.326 align:middle
So, so that's what-that's
what we did.

45:20.326 --> 45:23.766 align:middle
That's what, together,
the economy accomplished.

45:24.246 --> 45:27.026 align:middle
So I don't see any reason to
think that we're looking at,

45:27.026 --> 45:29.656 align:middle
at a replay of the '70s
or anything like that.

45:29.656 --> 45:32.686 align:middle
You know, inflation-underlying
inflation is, is, you know,

45:32.686 --> 45:35.966 align:middle
still running in the 2s,
with probably a little bit

45:35.966 --> 45:37.866 align:middle
of a pickup associated
with tariffs.

45:38.856 --> 45:40.686 align:middle
So I, I don't think
we're facing-I,

45:40.686 --> 45:42.106 align:middle
I wouldn't say we're in,

45:42.106 --> 45:44.466 align:middle
in a situation that's
remotely comparable to that.

45:44.586 --> 45:45.096 align:middle
JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

45:45.096 --> 45:48.766 align:middle
And last month, the idea of
a DOGE dividend was proposed,

45:48.766 --> 45:50.576 align:middle
which would send $5,000 checks

45:50.636 --> 45:52.706 align:middle
to every taxpayer
from DOGE savings.

45:52.946 --> 45:55.376 align:middle
President Trump and Elon
Musk have supported this.

45:55.436 --> 45:58.256 align:middle
There's reports there could be a
bill introduced on Capitol Hill.

45:58.516 --> 46:02.786 align:middle
What impact might that have on
household savings and spending

46:03.356 --> 46:06.416 align:middle
in terms of your growth and,
and outlook for inflation?

46:06.416 --> 46:06.716 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:06.716 --> 46:09.316 align:middle
You know, I-it's not-it's not
appropriate for me to speculate

46:09.316 --> 46:12.986 align:middle
on political ideas or, or
fiscal policy, for that matter.

46:12.986 --> 46:14.506 align:middle
So I'm going to-I'm going
to pass on that one.

46:14.506 --> 46:14.796 align:middle
Thank you.

46:14.796 --> 46:15.396 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

46:15.886 --> 46:18.036 align:middle
Daniel. DANIEL AVIS.

46:19.886 --> 46:20.516 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

46:20.516 --> 46:22.196 align:middle
Daniel Avis from
Agence France-Presse.

46:22.906 --> 46:24.346 align:middle
You mentioned that
tariffs are already having

46:24.346 --> 46:26.516 align:middle
at least some impact
on inflation.

46:26.516 --> 46:28.206 align:middle
I'm just wondering how
you and your colleagues

46:28.206 --> 46:30.156 align:middle
on the FOMC have been
thinking about the possibility

46:30.156 --> 46:32.116 align:middle
of retaliatory tariffs
from other countries,

46:32.356 --> 46:33.976 align:middle
especially with April
2 coming up.

46:34.376 --> 46:35.946 align:middle
Was it something you
considered during this meeting?

46:36.166 --> 46:37.346 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

46:37.416 --> 46:39.706 align:middle
So, since the very
beginning, we've had kind

46:39.706 --> 46:45.526 align:middle
of a placeholder-the staff has a
placeholder of-range of-really a

46:45.526 --> 46:48.126 align:middle
range of possible
outcomes from tariffs,

46:48.126 --> 46:49.546 align:middle
and from trade policy generally.

46:49.636 --> 46:52.126 align:middle
And they generally assume
full retaliation in those.

46:52.546 --> 46:54.436 align:middle
And so that's, that's kind
of baked into the numbers.

46:54.766 --> 46:57.426 align:middle
What happens-it will
be complicated,

46:57.426 --> 47:00.206 align:middle
and there will be some
retaliation and some not and,

47:00.206 --> 47:02.116 align:middle
and all that, but,
ultimately, they're trying to,

47:02.586 --> 47:04.866 align:middle
with the placeholder,
give us a broad sense

47:05.386 --> 47:07.136 align:middle
of what this might look like.

47:07.196 --> 47:09.596 align:middle
This-when we know-when we
actually know the specifics,

47:09.596 --> 47:12.526 align:middle
we'll be able to, to have
still uncertain but, you know,

47:12.576 --> 47:14.006 align:middle
better-informed forecasts.

47:14.236 --> 47:16.966 align:middle
So, so, yes, that's in there.

47:17.066 --> 47:17.996 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

47:18.176 --> 47:19.176 align:middle
Last question goes to Jean.

47:20.066 --> 47:21.556 align:middle
JEAN YUNG.

47:21.836 --> 47:22.506 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

47:22.966 --> 47:24.636 align:middle
Jean Yung with MNI Market News.

47:24.946 --> 47:26.806 align:middle
Had a couple questions

47:26.806 --> 47:28.986 align:middle
on the balance sheet
change that you made.

47:29.516 --> 47:32.286 align:middle
So the minutes had initially
described your discussion

47:32.286 --> 47:34.176 align:middle
on slowing QT as temporary,

47:34.766 --> 47:36.596 align:middle
until the debt ceiling
is resolved,

47:36.766 --> 47:38.936 align:middle
but it-from what you said
earlier, doesn't sound

47:38.936 --> 47:42.086 align:middle
like there's a desire to
kind of regain the pace of QT

47:42.086 --> 47:44.266 align:middle
that we have at the
moment after the,

47:44.576 --> 47:46.326 align:middle
the debt ceiling is resolved.

47:46.326 --> 47:46.886 align:middle
Is that the case?

47:47.946 --> 47:48.666 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

47:48.666 --> 47:51.246 align:middle
Yeah, so we, we looked at
pausing, and we looked at,

47:51.246 --> 47:52.846 align:middle
at slowing, and, really,

47:52.846 --> 47:55.466 align:middle
people came together very
strongly behind slowing,

47:55.636 --> 47:58.136 align:middle
not pausing, for a
variety of reasons.

47:58.136 --> 48:00.946 align:middle
And so people really came to
be, you know, pretty strongly

48:00.946 --> 48:02.376 align:middle
in favor of, of this move.

48:02.376 --> 48:06.736 align:middle
It slows down the path and
probably lengthens it-you know,

48:06.736 --> 48:09.206 align:middle
doubles it, effectively,
by slowing it by half.

48:09.766 --> 48:11.806 align:middle
That's, that's-people
thought that's,

48:11.876 --> 48:13.266 align:middle
that's a good place to be.

48:13.266 --> 48:17.826 align:middle
It'll, it'll, you know, help
us assure that this path is,

48:17.936 --> 48:20.136 align:middle
is a smooth one as we get
closer and closer to that.

48:20.136 --> 48:21.266 align:middle
So that's how that came about.

48:21.266 --> 48:21.716 align:middle
JEAN YUNG.

48:21.916 --> 48:24.306 align:middle
Well, I guess my question
is more, was that meant

48:24.306 --> 48:26.746 align:middle
to be a temporary measure during
the debt ceiling episode, or-?

48:26.746 --> 48:27.536 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

48:27.726 --> 48:30.296 align:middle
You know, it was-it was
actually-it was the,

48:30.296 --> 48:32.286 align:middle
the TGA flows-Treasury
General Account flows

48:32.286 --> 48:33.786 align:middle
that got us thinking about this.

48:34.196 --> 48:36.276 align:middle
But the more we thought about
it, we came around to this.

48:36.836 --> 48:39.276 align:middle
And, and, you know, it, it
is-yes, it was provoked.

48:39.426 --> 48:42.086 align:middle
The original discussion
was provoked by that.

48:42.086 --> 48:43.806 align:middle
But I think what we
came up with, though,

48:44.156 --> 48:45.866 align:middle
was broader than that
and different than it.

48:45.866 --> 48:49.656 align:middle
It does address that issue,
but it really is also-it fits

48:49.656 --> 48:52.736 align:middle
in really nicely with our
principles and our plans,

48:52.736 --> 48:53.836 align:middle
and the things we've
done before,

48:53.836 --> 48:55.076 align:middle
and the things we've
said we would do.

48:55.596 --> 48:58.086 align:middle
So that's why I-you know,
pretty, pretty strong support.

48:58.516 --> 49:01.496 align:middle
I'd just say it's, it's nothing
to do with monetary policy,

49:01.966 --> 49:03.746 align:middle
nothing to do with the
size of the balance sheet.

49:03.746 --> 49:05.806 align:middle
It's just kind of a
commonsense adjustment

49:05.806 --> 49:07.876 align:middle
as you get closer and closer.

49:08.396 --> 49:09.716 align:middle
Let's slow down a
little bit again.

49:09.946 --> 49:11.796 align:middle
And, that way, we'll be
more and more confident

49:11.796 --> 49:13.326 align:middle
that we're getting
where we need to get.

49:13.386 --> 49:15.406 align:middle
You can take your
time getting there.

49:15.406 --> 49:17.406 align:middle
You know, we're shrinking the
balance sheet every month,

49:18.086 --> 49:24.426 align:middle
and we think that's-we think
it was a good play and,

49:24.766 --> 49:41.356 align:middle
as I mentioned, well supported.

49:41.356 --> 49:43.306 align:middle
Thanks very much.

